A||N||11||ToddAsh||Highway Handling||||5||
Z||000000||ToddAsh||11-04-2000||09:58 PM||||Just bought my '85 V6 SE with 14,000 miles and I love it except for a BAD handling problem.I replaced shocks & struts,back tires and alignment.Car sways badly at 60MPH and above,feels like the rear is steering. Will a rear sway bar fix this? Need help bad,just got back from a 150 mile drive and it was real disappointing. Other than this it's the most fun car I've ever driven. Thanks,Todd||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||11-04-2000||10:16 PM||||If the car really only has 14K on  it, I would suspect that the alignment was not done properly.  Did you get a computer printout of the before and after alignment settings?  If so post the numbers and someone can see if they are withing specs.  The Fiero can be very sensitive to alignment settings, particularly rear toe in.  <P>I doubt a rear sway bar will help your problem at all.  I have a stock 85 SE 4 cyl that I was running at up to 85 MPH during the recent RFTH event and everything was well planted and handled fine.<P>You might want to carefully check to see that averthing in your suspension is tight and that you don't have any play anywhere.  When I got my daughter's 87 coupe the rear end felt like it was steering itself.  Wass very unsettling!  We discovered a bad inner toe link at the left rear wheel was causing the toe in to change all by itself.  With that fixed and a proper alignment, the car handles great.  I did a complete front suspension rebuild during the restoration.  The car has 165K on the clock right now.<P>Good luck...<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000002||ToddAsh||11-04-2000||10:25 PM||||Thanks,here's my alignment specs.<BR>Rear toe .05 left & right<BR>Rear camber left-.9  right-.7<BR>Front toe .15 left & right<BR>Front camber left .3  right .<BR>Front caster left 3.7  right 3.5<BR>Car handles badly,everything looks tight and new.DESPARATE. Todd||||reg||5
Z||000003||stimpy||11-04-2000||10:31 PM||||I concur with the opinion that the lack of a swaybar i not your problem. I have 146k miles on my 86 GT and experience nothing but bliss as I take it into ticketable speeds. I have no suspension mods, other then the fact that the original owner (who had it for the first ten years and 117k miles) took exceptional care of it and replaced parts as they began to wear.  The only handling issues I have noticed is a lift in the front as I exceed 110 mph.  As I enjoy having a drivers license and the distinction of never being arrested, I avoid doing that.<P>I think taking it to a good, modern alignment shop and have the alignment rechecked will do you a world of good.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bens88GT||11-04-2000||11:09 PM||||My 88GT has the same problem; the rear feels like it's steering itself. Bob, how did you figure out that you had a bad rear toe link? Did it look bad, or did you have to test it somehow?<P>Thanks,<BR>Ben||||reg||5
Z||000005||2xsess||11-04-2000||11:25 PM||||Since it has so low miles on it, I assume it has been sitting somewhere. I'd check the cradle bushings out, they might be dry rotted from sitting so long. That would also cause the rear to feel that way. Putting poly bushings would help that.<P><BR>Good Luck and welcome to the Forum. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000006||Oreif||11-04-2000||11:43 PM||||Here is a list of things to check.<BR>Cradle bushings and mounts.<BR>Control arm bushings (front and rear)<BR>Tire air pressure<BR>Tie rods (front and rear)<BR>ball joints (front and rear)<BR>broken or rotted out sway bar/bushings on the front.<BR>With the low miles bushings could be dry rotted just enough to cause excessive flex in the control arms while driving.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||ToddAsh||11-05-2000||12:05 AM||||Thanks everyone.I'll be checking out everything tomorrow.The funny thing is that the car will be fine for miles and then all of a sudden it kind of crab back and forth.I just went out to the garage and jacked up the car and checked the tie rod ends.Feel tight. I bought the car from the 79 year old original owner.He drove the car 6 miles every Saturday for 15 years to keep the car running.It's really brand new.I hope I find some dried out bushings tommorrow.Thanks again.Todd||||reg||7
Z||000008||jammer||11-05-2000||08:46 AM||||find a long section of road , take a friend with you let him drive toward you at 40 mph or so. make sure the car is tracking straight. then have toe and chamber set, unless you know for sure it could have been in a crash.<P>------------------<BR>jammer||||reg||2
Z||000009||Getaway2k||11-05-2000||11:17 AM||||I had the same problem with an 86 GT that I had. Believe it or not, it turned out to be low air pressure in the tires.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||11-05-2000||11:43 AM||||Like 2xsess said, check the cradel bushings. I'd recomend replacing them even if they look good. The rubber ones, even when new, allow to much play in the cradel. movment of the cradel will change the whole rear alignment. (The cradel is the only place I always recomend polly or solid bushings.)<P>like Getaway2k said... do the obvious and check the tire pressure. If you don't have stock tire sizes you may need a couple pounds more or less than the door sticker says.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Bob Englert||11-05-2000||10:52 PM||||Benns88GT:<P>I found the bad inner tie rod by jacking up the rear end and shaking the tires in every direction.  Found the left rear tire and rim had excessive movement.  At first I thought it was the rear wheel bearing assembly (didn't check it carefully).  After replacing that and seeing no difference I started looking to see what was moving when I rocked the wheel.  Discovered the inner and outter tie rods moving as I rocked the wheel.  Since the inner tie rod is supposed to be firmly attached to the cradle, this was the problem.  The rod was bad at the joint with the cradle.  Ed Parks installed a used replacement when he installed the Grooms motor in me daughters coupe.  The car is very solid at any speed now.<P>Generally (but not always), you can check out the suspension by lifting the car and rocking the wheels.  There should not be any "Clunk-clunk" movement or noises at all.  I just found a bad right front inner tie rod on my second 87 GT that I was trying to get ready to take to Ed Parks.  The wheel moved around like the wheel bearing was loose.  After looking at what was moving I saw the excess movement of the tie rod at the rack.<P>The cradle bushings are also worth checking as other have said.<P>Good luck...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
